# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BrandFactory — server environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env` at the repo root and fill in what's needed. Loaded by
# `@brandfactory/server` via `packages/server/src/env.ts`; every key below
# appears in `EnvSchema` there. A drift-guard test in
# `packages/server/src/env.example.test.ts` fails the build the moment the
# schema widens without this file following.
#
# Frontend (`VITE_*`) env vars live in `packages/web/.env.example`; those
# are inlined into the client bundle and must not be secrets.
#
# The shipped defaults boot the path that exercises the most code with the
# fewest third-party accounts: `local` auth + `local-disk` storage +
# `native-ws` realtime + `openrouter` LLM. The only secret a contributor
# needs to fill in is `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`.
#
# `drizzle-kit` does not auto-load `.env` — export `DATABASE_URL` in your
# shell, or prefix the command (e.g. `DATABASE_URL=... pnpm -F
# @brandfactory/db db:migrate`).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres connection string. Matches the `postgres` service in
# `docker/compose.yaml`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://brandfactory:brandfactory@localhost:5432/brandfactory

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `local`    — bearer token is a user UUID; the seed script prints one.
# `supabase` — verifies JWTs against SUPABASE_JWKS_URL (set below).
AUTH_PROVIDER=local

# ── Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `local-disk` — writes blobs under BLOB_LOCAL_DISK_ROOT, server signs URLs.
# `supabase`   — proxies to Supabase Storage.
STORAGE_PROVIDER=local-disk

# ── Realtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only `native-ws` ships today; the enum widens when a second impl lands.
REALTIME_PROVIDER=native-ws

# ── LLM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Active provider + model. Workspace-level overrides (Settings UI) shadow
# these defaults per workspace.
LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter
LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6

# ── Blob local-disk config ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required when STORAGE_PROVIDER=local-disk.
BLOB_LOCAL_DISK_ROOT=./.data/blobs
# HMAC secret for signing read/write URLs. Long random string. Server-only.
BLOB_SIGNING_SECRET=change-me-in-prod
# Public base URL the server serves blobs from. Phase 4 mounts the
# verifying `/blobs/:key?sig=...` route here.
#
# **Relative in dev, and that is load-bearing.** Vite proxies `/api`, `/rt` and
# `/blobs` to :3001 so the browser sees one origin; an absolute
# `http://localhost:3001/blobs` opts the bytes back out of that. Reading still
# works (an `<img src>` is not CORS-gated) but `uploadBlob` PUTs with `fetch`,
# which preflights — so uploads fail with `net::ERR_FAILED` and nothing else.
# That was the state from 0.7.4 until 2E found it.
#
# Split-origin deploys (web on one host, server on another) need the absolute
# form — e.g. `https://api.example.com/blobs` — alongside CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS.
BLOB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=/blobs
# Hard cap on `PUT /blobs/:key` request body. Defaults to 25 MiB when
# unset; applied regardless of STORAGE_PROVIDER. 413 BLOB_TOO_LARGE is
# returned before the body is read.
# BLOB_MAX_BYTES=26214400

# ── Supabase (auth + storage share these) ────────────────────────────────────
# Required when AUTH_PROVIDER=supabase:
#   SUPABASE_JWKS_URL (and usually the JWT issuer/audience pair).
# Required when STORAGE_PROVIDER=supabase:
#   SUPABASE_URL + SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY + SUPABASE_STORAGE_BUCKET.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
# SUPABASE_JWKS_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json
# SUPABASE_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated
# SUPABASE_JWT_ISSUER=https://your-project.supabase.co/auth/v1
# SUPABASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=brandfactory

# ── LLM provider keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set the key matching LLM_PROVIDER above. OpenRouter is the default so
# new contributors can run end-to-end against any OpenRouter-listed model
# with a single account.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL defaults to https://openrouter.ai/api/v1.
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL defaults to http://127.0.0.1:11434 — override only if remote.
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# ── Brand research (Stage 3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one feature here that costs money per run, so it is OFF by default.
# `none` builds a provider that refuses; the UI reads the absence and does not
# offer research at all, rather than offering it and failing.
#
# Measured: 3A $0.377 / 4.0 min · 3G $0.4157 / 5.2 min · vendor ceiling 3–15 min.
# The create dialog and the in-flight UI both state ≈$0.40 and 3–15 minutes.
#
# ── How to turn it on locally (watch a real run) ────────────────────────────
# 1. Postgres up, `.env` loaded, `pnpm dev`.
# 2. Set:
#      RESEARCH_PROVIDER=perplexity
#      PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=pplx-...
# 3. Restart the server (env is read at boot).
# 4. Create a brand with a website and leave "Research this brand" checked,
#    *or* open an existing brand hub → rail footer → Research this brand.
# 5. Stay on the hub or leave it: the rail and the header chip both report
#    the run. Drafts + a report conversation land when COMPLETED.
#
# Production (Fly): still OFF unless you set the secret + env. Research is
# never implied by a website URL alone — the user must opt in (create dialog
# checkbox or rail action). Example:
#   fly secrets set PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=pplx-...
#   fly secrets set RESEARCH_PROVIDER=perplexity
# (or set RESEARCH_PROVIDER in fly.toml [env] once you intend to bill runs).
RESEARCH_PROVIDER=none
# Required when RESEARCH_PROVIDER=perplexity.
# PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=pplx-...
# RESEARCH_MODEL defaults to sonar-deep-research.
# RESEARCH_MODEL=sonar-deep-research
# Decision 12's two guards, per workspace, both enforced before the outbound
# call. Counts rather than a dollar cap: real spend is known only after a run
# finishes, so a dollar cap discovers the overrun too late to prevent it.
# RESEARCH_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_WORKSPACE=2
# RESEARCH_MAX_JOBS_PER_DAY=10
# How long a run may stay IN_PROGRESS before it is closed as stale. A row that
# never reaches a terminal state blocks its brand and holds a slot in the
# active cap above, both permanently. The vendor documents 3-15 minutes.
# RESEARCH_JOB_MAX_MINUTES=60
# Guideline auto-fill, Path S: when a brand has no completed research report,
# the per-section sparkle runs one targeted, search-grounded completion pinned
# to the brand's own domain. Measured (Phase A spike): ~$0.01 and 5-7 seconds
# per section on sonar-pro. Uses the same provider + key as above.
# RESEARCH_SECTION_MODEL=sonar-pro
# Per-workspace rolling-24h cap on those searches. Only searches count —
# filling from an existing report spends no vendor money.
# RESEARCH_SECTION_MAX_PER_DAY=20

# ── HTTP server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT=3001
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── CORS (split-origin deploys only) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated allowlist of browser origins that may call the API +
# connect to `/rt`. Unset → no CORS headers (single-origin dev via Vite's
# proxy works without this). Set → gates both HTTP `cors()` and the WS
# upgrade `Origin` header with exact-match.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,https://staging.example.com

# ── Mission Passport (identity, orgs, structure, roles) ──────────────────────
# Plan: docs/executing/passport-sync-consumer-plan.md
#
# Passport is the source of truth for organisations, structure units (entity /
# brand / outlet), platform users, memberships, roles and entitlements.
# BrandFactory projects those facts into `passport.*` read-model tables from a
# webhook feed and reads them on its request path.
#
# ALL OF IT IS OFF WHEN UNSET, and that is a supported state: the app boots and
# behaves exactly as it did before. What is refused at boot is a HALF-set
# configuration, because each half fails silently on its own — a webhook secret
# without API credentials projects every org and can identity-link nobody, and
# API credentials without a webhook secret derive access from a projection that
# never receives an event.
#
# Two variables you may be looking for do NOT exist, and `loadEnv` refuses them
# by name rather than ignoring them:
#
#   PASSPORT_ORG_ID     — a consumer is multi-org from day one. Resolve the org
#                         from the acting user's membership; a configured org
#                         reached from a query silently drops other orgs' events.
#   PASSPORT_UNIT_SCOPE — placement is read from GET /apps/me/registry at
#                         startup (`unit_scopes` + `role_cascade`). The singular
#                         form is retired, and a local copy of a fact Passport
#                         owns drifts the moment an operator re-scopes the app.

# The BACK-CHANNEL host. Do not give it a trailing slash: `${url}/api/v1/...`
# then yields `//api/v1/...` and Passport answers a flat 404.
# PASSPORT_API_URL=https://passport-api.example.com
# `X-API-Key` on every App API call, and the OAuth client-secret on the
# hosted-login code exchange. Server-only, never logged.
# PASSPORT_API_KEY=
# This app's `app.id` in Passport's registry — also its OAuth `client_id`.
# PASSPORT_APP_ID=

# HMAC secret for `POST /webhooks/passport/sync`. Unset, the receiver refuses
# with 503 rather than accepting anything: an unset secret must never mean
# "trust every caller".
# PASSPORT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# A second accepted secret, for the overlap window of a rotation. Without it a
# rotation is a hard cutover.
# PASSPORT_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREV=

# Guards the nightly reconciliation trigger. The endpoint refuses to run when
# this is unset rather than running unauthenticated, so an unconfigured
# scheduler cannot look like a working one.
# PASSPORT_RECONCILE_SECRET=

# ── Hosted login (Passport-issued sessions) ──────────────────────────────────
# Passport's Supabase project — the issuer we verify Passport-issued tokens
# against. THIS is what activates SSO, not the flag below: with it empty the
# login router sends everyone down the app-native branch, which is why local and
# CI need no flag. Naming the wrong project presents as sessions that expire
# immediately rather than as a configuration error.
# PASSPORT_SUPABASE_URL=https://<passport-project>.supabase.co
# The BROWSER redirect target (`/authorize`). A DIFFERENT host from
# PASSPORT_API_URL — Passport's frontend, not its API.
# PASSPORT_DASHBOARD_URL=https://passport.example.com
# Must equal the URI on Passport's PER-APP callback allow-list byte for byte
# (Apps -> the app -> Sign-in callbacks). Not the Supabase project-level
# Redirect-URL list. A trailing slash or case difference is a different URI and
# the exchange is a flat 403.
# PASSPORT_SSO_CALLBACK_URL=https://app.example.com/api/auth/passport/callback
# A break-glass switch, not a rollout toggle — in every environment it is on.
# Set it false only to kill SSO during an incident while keeping the config in
# place. Only `true` and `false` are accepted.
# PASSPORT_SSO_ENABLED=true
# The FRONTEND origin. Both hosted-login redirects leave the backend for the web app,
# so this must be absolute: unset, the redirect is relative, the browser resolves it
# against the BACKEND origin, and the person lands on a 404 with a live session in the
# URL fragment. Nothing errors. Required once PASSPORT_SUPABASE_URL is set.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://app.example.com
